package httpserver

import (
	nethttp "net/http"
	"strings"

	"__module__/internal/httpx"
)

// APIPrefix is the default mount prefix for the built-in API and the business
// modules (see ModulePrefix).
//
// It is the one place the HTTP surface's default top-level path is declared.
// Change it (for example to "/api/v2") to version the surface; the generated
// server graph mounts the API and the modules from this constant (through
// ModulePrefix), so the change survives every later `weld add`. A module declares
// its paths relative to its mount, so it follows the prefix without an edit.
const APIPrefix = "/api"

// ModulePrefix returns the URL prefix a business module is registered under.
//
// The generated server graph mounts every module through this function, so the
// project owns the mapping from a module to its URL space without editing the
// regenerated graph. The default keeps a module under the versioned API prefix
// (APIPrefix/<name>). Return "" for a module that declares its own full paths —
// for example one whose public routes live at /v1/thing and whose administrator
// routes live at /admin/v1/thing, which do not fit a single APIPrefix/<name>
// mount; the module then owns its complete paths and the graph mounts it at the
// root.
//
// The name is the module's package name (the module's Name constant). Version
// the whole surface in one place here, or special-case one module, without
// touching internal/di/di.go.
func ModulePrefix(name string) string {
	return APIPrefix + "/" + name
}

// PolicyAdmin is the name of the administrator route policy. A module marks its
// administrator routes with httpx.Policy(PolicyAdmin) and implements no check
// itself; the generated server graph resolves the name once, so every module
// shares one guard.
const PolicyAdmin = "admin"

// NamedPolicy binds a policy name to the middleware that enforces it.
type NamedPolicy struct {
	Name       string
	Middleware httpx.Middleware
}

// Policies returns every named policy the generated server graph applies, in
// order.
//
// The graph declares this function as a Loom provider and loops over its result,
// so adding a policy — a "login" policy that requires a signed-in caller next to
// the administrator one, say — is an edit here, in this stable project file,
// rather than an edit to the regenerated internal/di/di.go. Each policy can take
// the dependencies it needs as parameters, because Loom constructs it; a policy
// that returns an error fails startup rather than serving its routes unguarded.
//
// A module references a name with httpx.Policy(name) and a name no route uses yet
// is harmless, so the set can grow ahead of the routes that need it.
//
// Two edits go with a new policy: a module that requires it, and the composition
// test's own list in internal/di/di_test.go. That test applies its list instead of
// this provider, so a name it omits makes the composed test panic — which is the
// fail-closed behavior working, not a bug to work around.
func Policies() ([]NamedPolicy, error) {
	return []NamedPolicy{{Name: PolicyAdmin, Middleware: AdminGuard()}}, nil
}

// AdminGuard is the project's administrator policy: the guard the generated
// server graph registers for every module route marked httpx.Policy("admin").
//
// The graph declares this function as a Loom provider and injects its result, so
// the guard can take the dependencies a real check needs: add a parameter (for
// example the pool after `weld add db`) and Loom constructs it, with no edit to
// the regenerated graph. It is an ordinary net/http middleware: it runs before
// the handler and before the request body is bound, and it may read a header,
// verify a JWT or a session, or look a role up in the database. It rejects by
// writing httpx.Error and returning, and it passes by calling next.ServeHTTP,
// optionally carrying the caller's identity on the request context for a handler
// to read. Return an (httpx.Middleware, error) pair to fail startup when its own
// configuration is unusable.
//
// A policy name the graph never defines panics at startup, so a route can never
// serve unguarded because of a typo.
//
// The body below is a placeholder that accepts any non-empty bearer token, so a
// generated project compiles and its tests exercise the rejection path. It does
// not authenticate anyone: replace it, do not build on it.
func AdminGuard() httpx.Middleware {
	return func(next nethttp.Handler) nethttp.Handler {
		return nethttp.HandlerFunc(func(w nethttp.ResponseWriter, r *nethttp.Request) {
			authorization := r.Header.Get("Authorization")
			if !strings.HasPrefix(authorization, "Bearer ") || strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimPrefix(authorization, "Bearer ")) == "" {
				httpx.Error(w, nethttp.StatusUnauthorized, "authentication required")
				return
			}
			next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
		})
	}
}
